<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    The same lamp on a paper plate.

    ── WHY THERE ARE TWO ICONS ──────────────────────────────────────────────

    Asked for: "when you change theme, I want you to change the app icon too."
    Right, and it is the one piece of the app that lives *outside* the app — a
    warm near-black tile in a tray of light ones is the only thing about
    Lamplight that cannot follow the setting on its own.

    ── AND WHY THE FOREGROUND IS NOT SHARED. ISSUE 6. ───────────────────────

    It was, and that was the bug. This file swapped the *plate* to warm paper
    and went on pointing at the dark icon's foreground, whose shade is
    `dark.inkPrimary` — a near-white #F2F0EA. Near-white ink on #FAF9F5 paper is
    about 1.1:1: a white lamp on a white ground, with nothing to see. Reported
    in exactly those terms, and correctly.

    The mark is unchanged. What changed is which end of the palette draws it:
    `light.inkPrimary` for the shade and the light palette's deeper amber for
    the glow, both of which already existed and are verified in
    `08-design/CONTRAST-REPORT.md`. The monochrome layer is still shared,
    because Android throws its colour away and uses only the alpha.

    Switching between them is an activity-alias swap — see AndroidManifest.xml
    and IconSwitcher.kt, and note the ordering rule there, which is the whole
    of the risk.
-->
<adaptive-icon xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <background android:drawable="@drawable/ic_launcher_background_light" />
    <foreground android:drawable="@mipmap/ic_launcher_foreground_light" />
    <monochrome android:drawable="@mipmap/ic_launcher_monochrome" />
</adaptive-icon>
